{"id":"liquid_meshing","compound_name":"Simulation::MPM::liquid_meshing","source_path":"liquid_meshing/liquid_meshing.json","category":"Liquid","author":"Autodesk","description":"A graph that meshes fluid particle simulations","documentation_file":"liquid_meshing_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Liquid Meshing\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses the `points_to_liquid_surface` compound to mesh fluid points from a simulation or cache.\n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nThe inputs for this graph are the MPM fluid source and a spherical collider.\n\n### How it works\n\n- The graph is setup with a very simple MPM fluid example, constrained by a sphere.  These are the `create_collider` and `MPM_fluid` sections of the graph.\n\n- Once the points have been simulated, they are passed to the `points_to_liquid_surface` compound.  The compound voxelizes the input points geometry to a level set (signed distance field). This level set is then refined with dilation, smoothing, and erosion.  Please refer to the info-tab of the `points_to_liquid_surface` for more in-depth help.\n\n- The level set is connected to the `proxy` output of the terminal, and it is recommended to use this while working as it gives a faster result than the mesh\n\n- The resulting mesh is output to the `final` output of the terminal, after having a shader applied, for rendering.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","mpm":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":16,"connections":19,"values":142,"compounds":1},"node_types":{"Rendering::Materials::standard_surface_mat":2,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_cube":1,"Simulation::MPM::mpm_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::MPM::simulate_mp