rd_surface_mat3.emission_color","type":"Math::float3","value":{"x":"0f","y":"0f","z":"0f"}}],"compounds":[{"path":"root","name":"Simulation::MPM::liquid_meshing","metadata":{"compoundIsGraph":"true","author":"Autodesk","category":"Liquid","description":"A graph that meshes fluid particle simulations","documentation":"liquid_meshing_docs_${language}.md","_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Constrain the Fluid\nBoth for the purposes of this example graph, and for a demonstration of collision, the fluid is constrained within a sphere mesh.  This is an extruded sphere and as such, we don't want to do the extrusion calculations on every frame, so a ***compute on frame*** compound is used to limit the calculating to the first frame."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Resolution\n\nThe lower this number, the finer the resolution of the fluid and the subsequent meshing becomes.\n\nLower values can get computationally intensive."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# MPM Simulation\nLike most other MPM Simulations, the fluid contains a source, a collider, solver settings and a material.\n\nThe ***source_mpm_fluid*** compound contains all the settings to control fuild behavior. These are explained well in the documentation that's available on the Info tab\"."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***points_to_liquid_surface***\n\nThis is the compound that turns your points into a mesh.  Unlike other, simpler meshing techniques like ***points_to_volume*** used with ***volume_to_mesh***, this compound allows the manipulation of the intermediate level set with controls for dilation, erosion and smoothing. \n\nPlease refer to in the documentation that's available on the Info tab for more details.\n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Output\nThe diagnostic tab contains the simulation particle output, the proxy tab contains the ***points_to_liquid_surface*** level set and the final tab contains the resulting mesh, ready to be rendered."}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create Collider"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Master Resolution"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"MPM Fluid"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Points to Liquid Surface"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Shade and output"}]},"ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"node_count":16,"connection_count":19,"value_count":142}],"teaching_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Constrain the Fluid\nBoth for the purposes of this example graph, and for a demonstration of collision, the fluid is constrained within a sphere mesh.  This is an extruded sphere and as such, we don't want to do the extrusion calculations on every frame, so a ***compute on frame*** compound is used to limit the calculating to the first frame."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Resolution\n\nThe lower this number, the finer the resolution of the fluid and the subsequent meshing becomes.\n\nLower values can get computationally intensive."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# MPM Simulation\nLike most other MPM Simulations, the fluid contains a source, a collider, solver settings and a material.\n\nThe ***source_mpm_fluid*** compound contains all the settings to control fuild behavior. These are explained well in the documentation that's available on the Info tab\"."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***points_to_liquid_surface***\n\nThis is the compound that turns your points into a mesh.  Unlike other, simpler meshing techniques like ***points_to_volume*** used with ***volume_to_mesh***, this compound allows the manipulation of the intermediate level set with controls for dilation, erosion and smoothing. \n\nPlease refer to in the documentation that's available on the Info tab for more details.\n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Output\nThe diagnostic tab contains the simulation particle output, the proxy tab contains the ***points_to_liquid_surface*** level set and the final tab contains the resulting mesh, ready to be rendered."}]}